# UX/UI rules

Audited 2026-09-22 at commit `890bb36` (56 pages, 43 deals), against the local build and
https://thestash.deals, in headless Chrome at 1440px and 390px. Performance numbers are from a
390px run under 4x CPU throttling and ~1.6 Mbps / 150 ms RTT unless marked *production*. Contrast
ratios are computed from the `:root` tokens in `src/site.css`. Every rule cites the measurement that
motivated it; re-measure before assuming the numbers still hold.

Rules marked **[build]** are enforced by the guardrail block at the end of `build()`.
SEO, structured data, titles and indexability live in `.cursor/rules/seo.mdc` — not here.

## The one thing to internalise

**The automated accessibility score on this site is blind, and it is blind specifically about
colour.** Lighthouse reports accessibility **100/100** on both the local build and production.
It is wrong. `body` paints a radial gradient and `.card` paints a linear gradient, so axe-core
cannot resolve a background colour for almost anything: it returns **225 of 226 text nodes as
"incomplete — background color could not be determined due to a background gradient"** and only
1 real violation. That one violation is the sidebar count on the Curated link, caught *only*
because "10" is two characters wide — the single-digit counts next to it are identically broken
and get skipped as "content is too short to determine if it is actual text content".

So: never treat a green Lighthouse run as evidence about this palette. Compute the ratio.

## Tier 1 — accessibility (measured WCAG 2.2 AA failures; fix these first)

- **Check a text colour against the surface it actually sits on, not against `--bg`.** This is the
  root cause of every contrast failure below: `--muted #a89070` scores **6.11:1 on `--bg #0f1410`
  and passes**, which is why it was never noticed, but the same token scores **3.78:1 on `--panel
  #2f3d2a`** (sidebar) and **3.10:1 on `--panel-2 #3d4a32`** (top of the card gradient). Six
  selectors fail AA: `.was`, `.merchant time`, `.cat-link` and `.fine-note` at 3.10:1,
  `.nav-link .count` at 3.78:1, and `.crumbs` current item plus `.tag-chip .count` at 4.36:1
  (`--faint #8c775f` on `--bg`). **[build]** — the pairing table records each ratio as a floor and
  caps known failures at three, so a regression or a newly declared pairing fails the build.
- **A muted token has to clear 4.5:1 on the lightest surface it lands on.** `--muted` needs to reach
  about `#c2b19a` to clear 4.5:1 on `--panel-2` (4.52:1) while staying 8.91:1 on `--bg`; `--faint`
  needs about `#8e7a62` (4.53:1). Two token edits close all six failures.
- **The struck-through `was` price and the `Listed <date>` stamp are content, not decoration.** They
  are the two lowest-contrast strings on the site at 3.10:1, and they are exactly the numbers a deal
  shopper compares. Do not demote price or recency metadata to `--faint` or `--muted`-on-panel.
- **Remove the off-canvas drawer from the tab order and the accessibility tree while it is closed.**
  At 390px the closed sidebar is only translated (`transform: translateX(-105%)`, `display: flex`,
  `visibility: visible`, no `inert`, no `aria-hidden`), so it still holds **13 focusable elements at
  x = -321px**. Tab stops 4 through 16 move focus to links nobody can see, with no visible focus ring
  anywhere on screen — SC 2.4.3 and 2.4.7. Gate it on the checkbox state, not on `transform` alone.
- **Give the Menu control button semantics and an expanded state.** It is a bare
  `<label class="menu-btn" for="nav-toggle">` with no `role`, no `aria-expanded`, no `aria-controls`,
  and the actual tab stop is the 1x1 clipped `#nav-toggle` checkbox (also axe's only landmark
  violation: it sits outside every landmark). Opening the drawer does not move focus into it and does
  not trap focus; `Escape` does close it, via `nav.js`.
- **Every interactive target must be at least 24x24 CSS px (SC 2.5.8, AA in WCAG 2.2).** `.cat-link`
  renders **22 px tall** on every card — 43 instances on the home page, measured 48x22, 90x22 and
  125x22 — and breadcrumb links render 37x21 and 40x21 with only 20 px of separation, so the
  spacing exception does not rescue them. `.card-title a` is 30 px, `.nav-link` 43 px and `.cta`
  47 px, so padding the two offenders is the whole fix.
- **Client-side filtering must announce itself.** Clicking `Used` rewrites `.result-count` from
  "43 deals · newest first" to "6 deals · Used" and hides 37 cards, and that element carries no
  `aria-live` and no `role="status"` — SC 4.1.3. The zero-match branch is worse: `nav.js` injects
  `<p class="empty filter-empty">No deals with that tag on this board.</p>` with no live region and
  without moving focus, so a screen-reader user gets silence and an empty grid. **[build]** — the
  markup contract (`role="group"` with a name, `<button type="button">` with `aria-pressed`, and the
  `.result-count` element `nav.js` rewrites) is asserted; the live region still has to be added.
- **Never colour a focus ring the same as the background of the thing it outlines.** `:focus-visible`
  draws `2px solid var(--brass)` (`#d4a017`) and `.cta` has `background: var(--accent)` — the same
  `#d4a017`, so the ring is **1.00:1** against its own button. It is only perceivable because
  `outline-offset: 3px` pushes it onto the card surface (3.97:1). That is a fragile pass; if the
  offset ever goes to zero the focus indicator disappears on all 43 primary CTAs.

## Tier 2 — performance and delivery

Production Lighthouse mobile is already strong — performance **99**, LCP **1.5 s**, TBT **0 ms**,
CLS **0.048**, 113 KiB. The rules below are about the two things that are actually wrong
(third-party tags) and the one metric that is drifting (layout shift).

- **Verify a third-party tag returns executable JavaScript before shipping it on every page.** The
  Impact Universal Tracking Tag was removed from the page shell. The media-partner account was
  declined, and the loader returned HTTP 403 with an XML AccessDenied body instead of JavaScript,
  so the browser blocked it and the tracking calls never ran. Do not inject that tag again. A
  future network tag ships only after its script URL returns executable JavaScript.
- **`script-src` must list every host the deployed site actually loads a script from.** Cloudflare
  Pages injects `static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js`, and `public/_headers` does not allow
  it, so it is blocked on **every page** with a console error. That is the whole reason production
  Lighthouse best-practices is **93** rather than 100, and it means the site collects no
  first-party analytics. The allow-list has to cover CDN-injected scripts, not just ours.
- **Preload the webfonts, or accept a guaranteed FOUT and the layout shift that comes with it.**
  Nothing in `<head>` is a `preload`, `preconnect` or `dns-prefetch`, so the three `woff2` files are
  discovered only when the stylesheet finishes parsing: CSS lands at 525 ms, the fonts are requested
  at **546 ms** and arrive at **1133–1184 ms**, while FCP is **668 ms**. That is a **465–516 ms**
  window of fallback text.
- **All of the measured layout shift is font swap, and it grows with the board.** CLS was 0.0253 at
  29 deals and is **0.0403** at 43, across three shifts at 1176/1226/1272 ms — exactly when the fonts
  land. The dominant one (+0.0359) is `.deal-grid` jumping from y=386 to y=360 as the condensed face
  narrows `.tag-filters` and `.result-count` above it; the rest is `.tag-chip` shrinking 176→137 px,
  `.topbar-mark` 204→154 px and the `.off` badge 83→73 px. Reserve space for these or preload.
- **Do not add a fourth webfont face, and justify the third.** The three faces total **73,492 B**,
  the largest render-affecting payload on the site. Barlow Condensed **600 is 22,308 B — 30% of the
  font payload — and renders five elements on the home page** (`.eyebrow`, `.rail-kicker` and three
  `.tag-chip .count` spans) against **239 elements at weight 700**. Dropping it removes a third of
  the bytes and one of three parallel requests. **[build]** — `font-display: swap` on every face,
  every `src` present in `dist/fonts/`, and a 76,800 B total budget.
- **Do not ship desktop-only decoration to mobile.** `brand/stash-deals-emblem.jpg` is **22,760 B —
  20% of the 111.9 KB mobile payload** and the second-largest resource. It is requested at 211 ms,
  *ahead of the fonts it competes with*, is served at 320x320 into a 116x116 box, carries `alt=""`,
  has no `loading` or `fetchpriority`, and at 390px sits off-canvas where nobody sees it until they
  open the drawer.
- **Fingerprint anything you want to cache for a long time.** CSS and JS are content-hashed and
  served `immutable, max-age=31536000`; the fonts are not hashed and sit at `max-age=2592000`, so
  they cannot get the same treatment without risking a stale pair. Same rule that produced the CSS/JS
  hashing applies to `/fonts/*` and `/brand/*`.
- **Keep `<img>` width and height on every image.** **[build]**

## Tier 3 — mobile, zoom and reflow

- **Write the drawer breakpoint in `em`, not `px`.** `@media (max-width: 860px)` cannot match when a
  user zooms text, but `--sidebar: 19rem` scales: at 1280px with 200% text zoom the sidebar takes
  **608px — 47.5% of the viewport** — and leaves 672px for content, while still refusing to collapse.
  An `em` breakpoint makes zoom fold the sidebar into the drawer, which is the behaviour users want.
- **Bound the listing page.** The home page is **15,433 px tall at 390px** — roughly 18 viewport
  heights for 43 cards — with **975 DOM elements** and no pagination or load-more, and the deal cap
  in `loadDeals()` was just raised from 30 to 48. Decide on paging before the ceiling is reached
  rather than after.
- **Keep the Curated rail reachable.** The sidebar's content is 901 px tall, so at **1440x800 and
  below** the rail (y 744–875) is out of view, the sidebar scrolls with no scroll affordance, and the
  site's one editorial differentiator is invisible on a common laptop. 12 nav links, a 116 px emblem
  and a tagline are what consume the space.
- 320px is clean — no horizontal overflow at 320px or 390px on any page type, `h1` 40 px, CTA
  254x47 — and the sticky topbar is 65 px, 7.7% of an 844 px viewport. Do not regress either.

## Tier 4 — conversion and scannability

- **Every card needs the same price signals in the same place.** 41 of 43 cards show three (`now` at
  2.35rem amber, struck `was`, `% off` pill). The two unpriced cards show one: `Sale page` at
  1.45rem, no `was`, no `% off` badge — even though the discount exists in the title ("up to 14%
  off"). The scanning eye anchors on the amber number and finds prose. Give the unpriced variant its
  own discount signal in the price slot.
- **Keep the invisible half of the CTA label.** All 43 primary buttons read "View deal" and nothing
  else; link purpose (SC 2.4.4) rests entirely on the `sr-only` " at `<merchant>` (opens a new tab)"
  suffix, and the visible `svg.ext` glyph is the only sighted cue that the click leaves the site.
  **[build]** — `target="_blank"`, `rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer"`, the `sr-only` suffix and
  the `.ext` glyph are asserted on every CTA on all 56 pages, not just the home page.
- **Show a breadcrumb on listing pages, not only on deal pages.** All 11 aisle and curated pages
  render no `.crumbs` at all, so an entry from search has no upward path except the sidebar — which
  on mobile is behind the Menu button.
- **Handle staleness in the UI, not just in the schema.** `priceValidUntil` is `posted + 30 days`
  and the oldest card is 16 days old, but nothing in the build hides or flags a card past its
  window, so a dormant `deals.json` keeps showing prices the merchant has already changed.
- **Do not render a control that cannot work.** With JavaScript off the drawer still opens (it is a
  CSS checkbox) but the three filter chips are inert buttons. Either degrade them to crawlable
  filtered URLs or do not paint them.
- **One `<h1>` per page, no skipped heading levels, and a working skip link.** **[build]** — both
  asserted across all 56 pages; the skip link matters more than usual here because it is the only
  way past 13 nav links by keyboard.

## Already correct — do not regress

`prefers-reduced-motion` resets the only two animated things in the stylesheet (`html`
`scroll-behavior` and the `.sidebar` transition) — **[build]** asserts that any newly animated
selector is also reset. Filter chips are real `<button type="button">` elements with `aria-pressed`,
so they are keyboard-operable and the filter state survives in `?tag=`. `Escape` closes the drawer.
Landmarks and `aria-current="page"` are in place. LCP is a text node (`p.lede`), not an image, so
there is no image-LCP problem to solve.

## Backlog, in priority order

1. Add `static.cloudflareinsights.com` to `script-src` when Cloudflare Web Analytics is enabled. Do not re-add the declined Impact tracking tag.
2. Lighten `--muted` to ~`#c2b19a` and `--faint` to ~`#8e7a62`; drop the known-failure cap to 0.
3. Make the closed drawer `inert`/`aria-hidden`, and give `.menu-btn` `aria-expanded`.
4. Pad `.cat-link` and the breadcrumb links to 24 px minimum.
5. Add `role="status"` to `.result-count` and a live region for the zero-match message.
6. Preload the three fonts; drop the Barlow Condensed 600 face.
7. Move the emblem behind a `min-width: 861px` fetch, or ship a 232px version.
8. Switch the drawer breakpoint to `em`.
9. Give unpriced cards a discount signal; add breadcrumbs to listing pages.
10. Decide the paging story before the 48-deal cap is hit.
